---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import ReviewPipeline from "../../components/marketing/ReviewPipeline.astro"
import { reviewPipelineAvailable } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="verified-completion">

          <p>
            Completion you can check, instead of a confident summary. Turn on LLM-as-a-judge and finishing stops
            being the agent's own opinion. Before it starts, the agent writes down the observable success criteria
            your request implies. After implementing, it has to exercise the work rather than describe it. Then the
            Judge, a read-only <a href="/docs/subagents#specialists">subagent specialist</a>, compares the original
            request, the criteria, the changed files and the captured test output. A FAIL names the specific repair and
            sends the agent back to work, up to three rounds.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            Opt-in, and it spends extra model calls on every prompt it covers. It cannot apply to the plan lane, which
            produces nothing to verify, or to the quick lane, which has no ability to spawn the judge at all.
          </p>

          <h2 id="then-code-review">Then code review</h2>
          {!reviewPipelineAvailable && (
            <p class="note">Coming soon. This setting is planned for an upcoming desktop release.</p>
          )}
          <ReviewPipeline id="then-code-review-diagram" tone="docs" />
          <p>
            Code review after each task is a separate desktop app setting, next to LLM-as-a-judge in Settings → General.
            It is off by default and appears once Vectorscope local reviews are turned on. The review needs a compatible
            Linux engine. When the setting is on, each task you send from the desktop app that changes files goes to
            Vectorscope once Vector is done. With the judge off, the review follows the task. With the judge on, the
            review waits until the judge's last round is done, then reviews the code as that round left it, whatever
            the verdict. If you stop the judge, the work it was checking isn't reviewed. A task that ends by asking you
            something is reviewed after you answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            The review reports findings and never edits your files. It covers all uncommitted changes in the
            repository, your own included, and runs on your Vectorscope model. A repository whose
            <code>.vector/review.json</code> picks another model or turns on the panel isn't reviewed automatically, and
            neither is a model with no price Vector can read. When the review finishes, its findings appear under Pull
            requests, Local reviews, and Vector reads them with your next message, so you can ask it to fix them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Each review costs extra model calls. On a priced model it stops at $2 or at what is left of the run's spend
            limit, whichever is lower, and at $0.50 when the run has no limit; it doesn't start with less than $0.10
            left. A finished task is reviewed once. While a review runs, the next finished task waits for it, then gets
            its own.
            Greetings, questions, plan mode before you approve the plan, subagents, and runs that were stopped, reverted
            or changed no files are never reviewed, and a review cut off by a restart doesn't report back.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
